The Five Principles:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa159\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Reward Clear Thinking: AI requires precise, well-understood requirements. If you cannot explain the task in one or two sentences, the AI will likely make incorrect assumptions. Think through your data models and requirements before prompting.\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use Non-Redundant Context: Avoid starting from scratch. Use project files like 
\f1 \cf2 CLAUDE.md
\f0 \cf2  to store persistent context, such as your tech stack, architectural patterns, and known anti-patterns, ensuring the AI aligns with your system's specific constraints.\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Shrink the Blast Radius: Break large features into smaller, specific, and verifiable tasks. Large AI-generated changes can introduce compounding errors that are difficult to debug; smaller tasks are easier to verify and secure.\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Build a Feedback Loop: Always include an objective signal, such as Test-Driven Development (TDD). Writing tests before the AI generates code helps catch subtle logic errors and edge cases early.\
\ls1\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	5	}\cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Protect Your Architectural Judgment: Maintain your role as the architect. Delegate repetitive work like boilerplate, documentation, and refactoring to AI, but keep human control over core business logic, API contracts, and security-sensitive decisions to ensure you understand your own codebase.}